course: dictionary
lesson: 7887
title: What is randomness?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Randomness means happening by chance, with no pattern or plan that tells you what comes next. A fair dice shows randomness: every number is equally likely, but you cannot say which one will come next. «Bots add some randomness, so even the same bot can answer differently twice.» The words "so even the same bot can answer differently" are the clue: randomness makes things change, even when the start is the same.
explain: Look for the phrase «an element of randomness», which means a small part of something is left to chance. Ask yourself: is the result decided by a plan, or by chance? To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: "Bots add some chance, so even the same bot can answer differently twice." It still makes sense, so chance is a good meaning.
explain: In everyday talk, "random" can also mean odd or unexpected, as in «that was so random!». This is informal, so it is more relaxed than the maths meaning. The context tells you which meaning the writer wants: in a maths or science text, it means chance; in a chat about silly jokes, it may mean odd.
fact: Randomness means happening by chance, with no pattern you can predict.
fact: A fair dice has a 1 in 6 chance of a six on every roll.
fact: A fair dice does not remember the last five rolls, so each roll is a new chance.
fact: Real randomness has streaks, so a run of the same result can still be random.
fact: Informal: «random» can mean strange or unexpected, as in «the randomness of his jokes».
exercise: In «Bots add some randomness, so even the same bot can answer differently twice», what does randomness make happen? | The answer is always the same | Nothing at all | *Answers can change, even from the same bot | The bot stops working
exercise: Which thing shows randomness in a maths lesson? | A set of rules that never change | *A fair dice, where you cannot say which number comes next | A plan of the week | A clock that is always right
exercise: Ellie says «that was so random!» about a silly joke. What does random mean here? | Happening by chance, like a fair dice | Very old | Made of metal | *Odd or unexpected
test: «A fair dice does not remember the last five rolls.» What does this tell you about each roll? | *Each roll is still a new chance | The dice is broken | Its rolls follow a pattern | The next roll is always a six
test: «Pressing "regenerate" gives a new story.» Why does the story change? | The story is always the same | *Randomness means the answer can be different each time | The bot is too slow to finish | The bot is counting the words
exam: Which best describes randomness? | *The quality of happening by chance, with no pattern | Something that is always the same | A plan that can be predicted | A fact that is hard to say
